   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.7",
    "topic_title": "Proteins",
    "stem": "A protein consists of a single polypeptide. A second version has the same amino acid sequence except that one amino acid with a hydrophobic R group is replaced by an amino acid with a charged R group. The second version has a different three-dimensional shape and reduced function. Which explanation best connects the replacement to these observations?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "The unchanged chain length means primary structure is unchanged, so the reduced function must be unrelated to protein structure.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Length–sequence confusion: Primary structure is the amino acid sequence, not merely the number of amino acids. Replacing one amino acid changes primary structure and can affect folding and function. Fix: Compare amino acid identities and order, not just chain length."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The replacement changes the hydrogen bonds between R groups that define secondary structure, reducing the protein’s function.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Backbone–R-group confusion: The hydrogen bonds defining alpha-helices and beta-sheets form between backbone atoms, not R groups. Changes in R-group interactions can alter tertiary structure and thereby affect function. Fix: Identify whether each interaction involves backbone atoms or R groups before assigning a structural level."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The replacement changes R-group interactions, altering the protein’s folding and the shape needed for its function.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Replacing a hydrophobic R group with a charged R group changes the interactions available during folding. These changes can alter tertiary structure, disrupting the shape required for the protein’s function."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The replacement changes quaternary structure by rearranging interactions within the single polypeptide, reducing the protein’s function.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Tertiary–quaternary confusion: Quaternary structure involves interactions between multiple polypeptides. The overall three-dimensional folding of this single polypeptide is its tertiary structure. Fix: Count polypeptide chains before identifying a change as quaternary structure."
     }
    ]
   },
