   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.7",
    "topic_title": "Proteins",
    "stem": "A water-soluble protein folds so that several amino acids with hydrophobic R groups cluster in its interior, away from the surrounding water. A mutation replaces one of these interior amino acids with an amino acid whose R group is ionic (charged). Which prediction best describes the most likely effect of this substitution?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "The protein's function would be unchanged, because a single substitution does not change the protein's primary structure.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This trap assumes one substitution is too small to matter. Primary structure is the amino acid sequence itself, so any substitution changes it, and a buried charged R group can alter folding and function. Fix: Ask whether the sequence changed, then trace sequence to folding to function."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The peptide bonds next to the new amino acid would break, splitting the polypeptide into two shorter chains.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This confuses R-group interactions with the backbone. Peptide bonds link the carboxyl of one amino acid to the amine of the next, and every amino acid has these groups regardless of its R group. Fix: Check whether a change involves the backbone or the R group before predicting its effect."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Only the secondary structure would be affected, because alpha helices and beta sheets are held together by interactions between R groups.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This misassigns the bonds behind each structural level. Secondary structure comes from hydrogen bonds between backbone atoms, while hydrophobic and ionic R-group interactions shape tertiary structure. Fix: Match each interaction type to its structural level before deciding which level changes."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The charged R group would disrupt hydrophobic interactions in the interior, altering the tertiary structure and likely impairing the protein's function.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "A charged R group does not interact favorably with the nonpolar cluster, so the interactions that stabilize the folded interior change. Tertiary structure depends on R-group interactions, so the 3D shape shifts, and because shape determines function, the protein may no longer work properly."
     }
    ]
   },
